From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 18:11:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 18/59] fix(refinery): prove the ff-merge landed before closing
 the bead as merged (gcp-p87)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-refinery-patrol's merge-push step closed a work bead with
merge_result=merged and a merged_sha while the branch was NOT merged and
nothing was pushed (live case winnow-ksyp). The failure is self-concealing:
it reports success, and the false merged_sha is a real, reachable commit on
the target, so reachability-based audits cannot detect it.

Two defects produced it, and neither was incidental. The abort depended
solely on `set -e`, which did not propagate through the refinery's execution
harness, so a failed `git merge --ff-only` fell through into the metadata
write and the close. And the push verification was tautological on exactly
that path: after a failed ff-merge the worktree HEAD is still the target tip,
pushing an unchanged HEAD is a successful no-op, and the guard compared the
old tip to itself. It asserted neither that the target advanced nor that it
advanced by this branch.

The merge script is now a `merge_ff_push` function that checks every command
on its own exit status (`set -e` is deliberately absent, and its absence must
not change behaviour) and, before any bead state is written, requires three
things read back from a fresh post-push fetch: the ff-merge succeeded, the
target ADVANCED off the SHA the merge was actually performed on, and TEMP_SHA
is reachable from the new tip. merged_sha is TEMP_SHA — the branch tip that
carried the bead, never a target tip. That second half addresses the other
known bad record, winnow-rp57, whose merged_sha named a different bead's
commit; both share the shape of bookkeeping recording a target tip instead of
the commit that carried the bead, so the ff invariant MERGED_SHA = TEMP_SHA
is asserted rather than assumed.

Losing the race is expected on any rig whose CI pushes to the target after
every merge, so the lost-race path re-rebases onto the new tip and retries,
bounded at three attempts, instead of falling through or escalating. A
conflicting re-rebase is aborted and the lane stops without mutating bead
state, letting the next patrol's rebase step run its existing rejection
handling. Under the approval gate the retry is disabled: an approval
authorizes one commit, so a lost race parks the bead awaiting review rather
than landing something no reviewer read.

Validation: gastown/tests/test_refinery_merge_push.sh extracts the block from
the shipped formula between its sentinels and drives it against real local
repositories — clean ff, lost race, conflicting re-rebase, approval-gated
park, a push that exits 0 without advancing the target, a target advanced by
someone else, a no-op ff, and a permanently rejected push. All nine were run
red against the pre-fix script first: it returns 0 with merged_sha set to the
racing commit while nothing of the branch is pushed, reproducing winnow-ksyp
exactly. The pack-asset and inference-gate contracts that pinned the old
text now pin the fix (no `set -e`, no tautological comparison, explicit merge
status, advance + ancestry assertions) and are red against the pre-fix
formula.

Follow-up, not in this bead: the city runs a PINNED copy of this pack
(sha:a4be2064 in city.toml), so landing on integration does not fix the live
refinery — a pin move is the Overseer's call. gcp-duy covers the related
already_merged predicate defect in the same file; it is untouched here and
the two do not overlap textually.
