+The metadata write happens only after the remote target ref is verified to
+have advanced, by this branch.
+
+`merge_ff_push` replaces a lane that reported `merged` while nothing was
+pushed (gcp-p87, live case winnow-ksyp). Two defects produced that, and both
+are structural rather than incidental — read them before touching this block:
+
+- **The abort depended solely on `set -e`.** The old script opened with
+  `set -e` and relied on it to stop after a failed `git merge --ff-only`. It
+  did not propagate through the refinery's execution harness, so execution
+  continued into the metadata write and `gc bd close`. A step that mutates
+  bead state must never depend on shell-option inheritance across a harness
+  boundary. Every command below is checked on its own exit status, and
+  `set -e` is deliberately NOT set — its absence must not change behaviour.
+- **The push verification was TAUTOLOGICAL on exactly that path.** After a
+  failed ff-merge the worktree HEAD is still the target tip; pushing an
+  unchanged HEAD is a successful no-op; and the old guard — MERGED_SHA read
+  from that HEAD, compared against a REMOTE re-read of the same target ref —
+  then compared the old tip to itself and passed. It never asserted the
+  target ADVANCED, nor that it advanced BY THIS BRANCH. The false
+  `merged_sha` it wrote is a real, reachable commit on the target, so
+  reachability-based audits cannot detect it.
+
+Three properties are therefore asserted before any bead state is written,
+each against state re-read after the push, never against the pre-merge
+snapshot:
+
+1. the ff-merge SUCCEEDED — checked on its exit status;
+2. `origin/$TARGET` ADVANCED — the freshly fetched post-push tip differs
+   from the tip the merge was actually performed on (`BEFORE_SHA`, captured
+   as a SHA and used as the worktree base, so there is no ref-vs-SHA gap);
+3. it advanced BY THIS BRANCH — `$TEMP_SHA` is reachable from that post-push
+   tip.
+
+`merged_sha` is `$TEMP_SHA`, the branch tip that carried the bead — never a
+target tip. That is the second half of the same evidence: winnow-rp57 was
+closed with a `merged_sha` naming a different bead's commit while its own
+work landed elsewhere. Both known bad records share one shape — merge
+bookkeeping recording a target TIP rather than the commit that carried the
+bead — so the ff invariant `MERGED_SHA = TEMP_SHA` is asserted explicitly
+rather than assumed.
+
+**Losing the race is expected, not an incident.** A rig whose CI pushes to
+the target after every merge (a deploy-overlay bump, a version stamp) races
+its own refinery on every cycle. On "Not possible to fast-forward" the lane
+re-rebases `temp` onto the new tip and retries, bounded at three attempts —
+it does not fall through, and it does not escalate. The retry rebases
+mechanically only: a conflicting re-rebase is aborted and the lane stops
+without mutating bead state, so the next patrol iteration re-runs the whole
+path and the **rebase** step's rejection handling deals with the conflict.
+
+Re-rebasing moves the branch, so the commit that lands is not byte-identical
+to the one **run-tests** checked. That is accepted for the mechanical case
+above and capped by the retry bound. It is NOT accepted under the approval
+gate: an approval authorizes one commit, so when `APPROVAL_REQUIRED` is 1 the
+lane refuses to re-rebase and returns the park status instead, and the next
+patrol iteration re-tests, re-publishes the head, and re-gates it.
 
